1
00:00:00,830 --> 00:00:04,459
I'm here to tell you a story about getting a cup of coffee.

2
00:00:04,459 --> 00:00:05,626
Or I should say,

3
00:00:05,626 --> 00:00:07,336
attempting to get a cup of coffee,

4
00:00:07,336 --> 00:00:09,005
because when I went to pay for it,

5
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:10,173
I couldn't.

6
00:00:10,173 --> 00:00:12,050
This [was] about a year and a half ago,

7
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:14,886
and there was nothing wrong with my card's chip,

8
00:00:14,886 --> 00:00:16,596
the store's reader was fine.

9
00:00:16,596 --> 00:00:18,973
The problem was with my bank account.

10
00:00:18,973 --> 00:00:20,850
It was empty.

11
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:25,146
Now a rational person would have just taken out another card.

12
00:00:25,146 --> 00:00:28,066
But in that moment, there was nothing rational about me.

13
00:00:28,066 --> 00:00:31,778
My chest tightened, my jaw clenched, my cortisol spiked.

14
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:34,781
I lost all awareness of a purse with a wallet

15
00:00:34,781 --> 00:00:36,949
and other pieces of plastic inside of it,

16
00:00:36,949 --> 00:00:39,452
and instead I just apologized profusely,

17
00:00:39,452 --> 00:00:40,661
walked out the door,

18
00:00:40,661 --> 00:00:43,247
and burst into tears.

19
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:45,500
Dramatic a tad.

20
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:47,877
But my reaction had very little to do

21
00:00:47,877 --> 00:00:50,213
with who I am in this moment --

22
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,006
a New York Times best selling author,

23
00:00:52,006 --> 00:00:54,592
a Pulitzer Prize finalist, a homeowner.

24
00:00:54,592 --> 00:00:58,471
And it had everything to do with who I once was:

25
00:00:58,471 --> 00:01:00,431
a working-class girl from South Brooklyn

26
00:01:00,431 --> 00:01:02,767
who didn't grow up with much.

27
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:06,104
Despite our nation's reputation as a land of opportunity,

28
00:01:06,104 --> 00:01:10,733
the truth is, upward social mobility in America is actually quite arduous,

29
00:01:10,733 --> 00:01:13,736
and less than 10 percent of people that start out like me,

30
00:01:13,736 --> 00:01:16,030
on the bottom rungs of the economic ladder,

31
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:18,116
ever managed to make it to the top.

32
00:01:18,116 --> 00:01:20,576
And somehow, I did.

33
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:24,247
So while it was a temporary problem paying for the coffee that day --

34
00:01:24,247 --> 00:01:26,541
I moved things around other accounts --

35
00:01:26,541 --> 00:01:30,545
that panic in my body was known from before.

36
00:01:30,545 --> 00:01:32,630
From 2009, when I was struggling

37
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,174
to get my small business through the Great Recession,

38
00:01:35,174 --> 00:01:36,425
from 1998,

39
00:01:36,425 --> 00:01:38,386
when I was working my way through college

40
00:01:38,386 --> 00:01:40,638
and there was no other account to pull from

41
00:01:40,638 --> 00:01:42,974
or [no] parent or relative to call.

42
00:01:42,974 --> 00:01:47,728
All the other times that I was living, like so many of my fellow Americans,

43
00:01:47,728 --> 00:01:50,148
from paycheck to paycheck.

44
00:01:50,148 --> 00:01:54,735
It's no secret that we are living in a time of wealth inequality

45
00:01:54,735 --> 00:01:56,863
not seen since the Gilded Age,

46
00:01:56,863 --> 00:01:59,240
but too often it's talked about

47
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,577
with the clinical distance of other people’s problems --

48
00:02:02,577 --> 00:02:06,247
an inconvenience that we wish somebody would do something about

49
00:02:06,247 --> 00:02:11,878
and far less discussed is the shared, urgent threat

50
00:02:11,878 --> 00:02:14,005
that this poses to our democracy.

51
00:02:14,005 --> 00:02:17,008
And if we don't start talking about income inequality

52
00:02:17,008 --> 00:02:19,093
like the civic issue it needs to be,

53
00:02:19,093 --> 00:02:21,846
we will surely slide further towards autocracy

54
00:02:21,846 --> 00:02:23,431
than we already have,

55
00:02:23,431 --> 00:02:25,766
maybe irrevocably so.

56
00:02:25,766 --> 00:02:27,435
Part of the problem is,

57
00:02:27,435 --> 00:02:29,896
even though we're a capitalistic society,

58
00:02:29,896 --> 00:02:33,399
we fascinatingly hate talking about money.

59
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:35,151
Like a lot of you,

60
00:02:35,151 --> 00:02:36,485
I have an Oura ring,

61
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:38,404
and the day of the coffee incident

62
00:02:38,404 --> 00:02:42,325
it told me I'd experienced an unusual amount of stress that day,

63
00:02:42,325 --> 00:02:47,663
and it gave me a few reasons -- work, bad sleep --

64
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:50,791
and maybe it’s because Oura is from Finland,

65
00:02:50,791 --> 00:02:52,835
but money wasn't an option.

66
00:02:52,835 --> 00:02:55,796
Which is unfortunate because in America,

67
00:02:55,796 --> 00:03:00,843
financial anxiety is linked to things like hypertension, depression

68
00:03:00,843 --> 00:03:03,095
and even reduced lifespan.

69
00:03:03,095 --> 00:03:04,764
But it all got me thinking:

70
00:03:04,764 --> 00:03:07,058
if money can impact our health,

71
00:03:07,058 --> 00:03:09,101
how does having money or not having money

72
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:12,021
impact our behavior and our thoughts?

73
00:03:12,021 --> 00:03:16,651
How does our socioeconomic situation impact the way we see politics

74
00:03:16,651 --> 00:03:17,860
and the way we vote?

75
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,070
And for the last year,

76
00:03:19,070 --> 00:03:20,613
I have been on a deep dive

77
00:03:20,613 --> 00:03:24,242
into the sociology and psychology of social class,

78
00:03:24,242 --> 00:03:27,119
and what I've discovered may surprise you.

79
00:03:27,119 --> 00:03:29,538
We're accustomed to thinking about our social class

80
00:03:29,538 --> 00:03:32,625
as little more than a matter of what's in our bank account,

81
00:03:32,625 --> 00:03:34,752
if you own a home or if you rent.

82
00:03:34,752 --> 00:03:38,547
But scientists have found that your social class of origin

83
00:03:38,547 --> 00:03:41,550
plays nearly as large a role in shaping your identity

84
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:44,804
as your race, gender or sexuality.

85
00:03:44,804 --> 00:03:48,516
And that children become aware of their own social class standing

86
00:03:48,516 --> 00:03:52,186
just a little later than they become aware of their race and gender.

87
00:03:52,186 --> 00:03:57,358
In fact, kids as young as seven can, with outrageous accuracy,

88
00:03:57,358 --> 00:04:02,405
correctly identify somebody else's social class just by looking at a photo.

89
00:04:02,405 --> 00:04:05,866
Your social class doesn’t just impact who you are --

90
00:04:05,866 --> 00:04:07,827
it impacts the way you think.

91
00:04:07,827 --> 00:04:10,204
It's one of the least discussed topics in America,

92
00:04:10,204 --> 00:04:13,833
and yet it's silently at play in so many aspects of our society.

93
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:15,543
Growing up with resources

94
00:04:15,543 --> 00:04:17,670
or growing up in scarcity

95
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:20,923
affects the way you see the world, your place in it,

96
00:04:20,923 --> 00:04:22,425
how you make decisions,

97
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:26,929
and how you see big abstract things like the role of government

98
00:04:26,929 --> 00:04:30,057
or the very idea of democracy itself.

99
00:04:30,057 --> 00:04:34,353
See, the well-resourced child is born into a place of security,

100
00:04:34,353 --> 00:04:36,439
and with their basic needs having been met,

101
00:04:36,439 --> 00:04:39,108
their parents can set out to getting them to thrive.

102
00:04:39,108 --> 00:04:43,821
And in America, that often means distinguishing oneself as an individual:

103
00:04:43,821 --> 00:04:45,197
exhibiting agency,

104
00:04:45,197 --> 00:04:47,575
the choices one makes about extracurriculars,

105
00:04:47,575 --> 00:04:48,826
what college you go to,

106
00:04:48,826 --> 00:04:52,621
your career and eventually your political party affiliation --

107
00:04:52,621 --> 00:04:55,041
these aren’t just matters of selection,

108
00:04:55,041 --> 00:04:57,335
they are statements of identity.

109
00:04:57,335 --> 00:05:00,546
And essentially, the way you present yourself to the world.

110
00:05:00,546 --> 00:05:04,342
So if you want to think about a typical middle class hero,

111
00:05:04,342 --> 00:05:06,135
it would be Neo from "The Matrix."

112
00:05:06,135 --> 00:05:08,471
He's just toiling away in that office job,

113
00:05:08,471 --> 00:05:12,350
and then his hero's journey begins when he makes a choice.

114
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:15,144
Will it be the red pill or the blue pill?

115
00:05:15,144 --> 00:05:17,605
Now for the child born into scarcity,

116
00:05:17,605 --> 00:05:20,649
which is almost 40 percent of children in America today,

117
00:05:20,649 --> 00:05:23,235
things are far more precarious.

118
00:05:23,235 --> 00:05:25,154
Security is not guaranteed,

119
00:05:25,154 --> 00:05:28,157
the world feels beyond your control because at any moment,

120
00:05:28,157 --> 00:05:29,742
something could present itself --

121
00:05:29,742 --> 00:05:31,744
a landlord, an unexpected medical bill --

122
00:05:31,744 --> 00:05:34,914
that throws everything into a tailspin.

123
00:05:34,914 --> 00:05:39,085
And so security is found in groups and conformity is encouraged.

124
00:05:39,085 --> 00:05:42,254
And expressing your own sense of agency is less important

125
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:46,133
than staying true to who you are in the face of constant adversity.

126
00:05:46,133 --> 00:05:50,096
Think about the cop John McClane in the blockbuster hit "Die Hard."

127
00:05:50,096 --> 00:05:53,099
He doesn't show up to that Christmas party looking for trouble,

128
00:05:53,099 --> 00:05:55,518
but he's going to fight for who he loves, his wife,

129
00:05:55,518 --> 00:05:58,229
and what he took a vow to defend, the law.

130
00:05:58,229 --> 00:06:00,523
These two groups don’t just think differently --

131
00:06:00,523 --> 00:06:02,024
they rarely converge.

132
00:06:02,024 --> 00:06:05,444
Living and working and worshiping separate from each other.

133
00:06:05,444 --> 00:06:10,074
Essentially only socializing with people of very similar circumstances.

134
00:06:10,074 --> 00:06:14,703
And so individual worldviews convene and harden into class cultures,

135
00:06:14,703 --> 00:06:17,540
cultures with very different ideas of what good governance

136
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:19,959
and leadership even looks like.

137
00:06:19,959 --> 00:06:21,961
Now for generations,

138
00:06:21,961 --> 00:06:26,757
America's humble middle class existed between these two groups.

139
00:06:26,757 --> 00:06:29,135
Like cartilage between bone.

140
00:06:29,135 --> 00:06:31,804
But as the top one percent of households

141
00:06:31,804 --> 00:06:35,975
has accrued the same amount of wealth as the bottom 90 percent combined,

142
00:06:35,975 --> 00:06:38,477
that cartilage has grown thin.

143
00:06:38,477 --> 00:06:43,482
And essentially, our middle class has been replaced by a comfort class:

144
00:06:43,482 --> 00:06:47,236
the children of the upper class, who on paper may not be rich,

145
00:06:47,236 --> 00:06:51,115
but who live with the security of a familial safety net,

146
00:06:51,115 --> 00:06:54,702
often working the kind of mid-pay, high-prestige jobs

147
00:06:54,702 --> 00:06:58,497
that influence a lot of American culture and policy.

148
00:06:58,497 --> 00:07:01,333
The result is almost a class apartheid,

149
00:07:01,333 --> 00:07:04,753
where a small group of people with generational wealth

150
00:07:04,753 --> 00:07:08,757
control a society whose culture they don’t fully understand.

151
00:07:08,757 --> 00:07:11,844
Our politicians, many of which are millionaires,

152
00:07:11,844 --> 00:07:14,013
their staffers and consultants,

153
00:07:14,013 --> 00:07:17,975
all come from a place where money has afforded them a sense of agency

154
00:07:17,975 --> 00:07:19,185
and control,

155
00:07:19,185 --> 00:07:21,228
such that they have a tendency

156
00:07:21,228 --> 00:07:23,689
to overestimate their influence on politics

157
00:07:23,689 --> 00:07:26,775
and the power of their own supporters' votes.

158
00:07:26,775 --> 00:07:28,486
The donor class,

159
00:07:28,486 --> 00:07:33,115
unfamiliar with that physiological panic that need can induce,

160
00:07:33,115 --> 00:07:36,660
tends to vote on social issues and look to support candidates

161
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:38,746
who mirror their values.

162
00:07:38,746 --> 00:07:42,500
But that need is still very, very real.

163
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:44,960
An anecdote.

164
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,213
In college, I was paired with a lovely roommate

165
00:07:47,213 --> 00:07:49,089
from an upper-middle class background,

166
00:07:49,089 --> 00:07:50,799
and one night, I decided to splurge

167
00:07:50,799 --> 00:07:52,927
and go and join her for an Italian meal out,

168
00:07:52,927 --> 00:07:55,513
carefully packing up my leftovers.

169
00:07:55,513 --> 00:07:57,598
But the next day, when I went to the fridge,

170
00:07:57,598 --> 00:07:59,725
my eggplant parm was gone.

171
00:07:59,725 --> 00:08:03,354
The victim of my roommate’s late-night drunchy.

172
00:08:03,354 --> 00:08:05,439
I flew into a rage and

173
00:08:05,439 --> 00:08:08,692
my poor, bewildered roommate could not understand.

174
00:08:08,692 --> 00:08:10,903
But to her it was an inconsiderate snack.

175
00:08:10,903 --> 00:08:15,115
And to me it was my next meal that I thought I'd already paid for.

176
00:08:15,115 --> 00:08:19,286
And now I was left to stretch my dollar another day.

177
00:08:19,286 --> 00:08:23,874
It's easy to judge people for voting based on the price of eggs

178
00:08:23,874 --> 00:08:26,877
when you are certain where your next meal is coming from.

179
00:08:26,877 --> 00:08:29,171
But for the financially struggling in America,

180
00:08:29,171 --> 00:08:31,215
politics, let alone democracy,

181
00:08:31,215 --> 00:08:35,261
can seem [like] a secondary concern and sometimes just an irrelevant one.

182
00:08:35,261 --> 00:08:37,805
Which is why you see so many lower-income Americans

183
00:08:37,805 --> 00:08:40,599
not participating in democracy in the first place.

184
00:08:40,599 --> 00:08:43,811
And equally concerning is that often, when they do,

185
00:08:43,811 --> 00:08:47,064
they tend to support authoritarian-leaning figures.

186
00:08:47,064 --> 00:08:49,066
From Hungary to Turkey,

187
00:08:49,066 --> 00:08:50,985
Senegal to the Philippines,

188
00:08:50,985 --> 00:08:55,614
researchers have found a consistent correlation between economic inequality,

189
00:08:55,614 --> 00:08:59,493
the decline of democracy and the rise of strongman leaders --

190
00:08:59,493 --> 00:09:03,455
the kinds of people that promise to fix working people's problems,

191
00:09:03,455 --> 00:09:06,375
often by sowing divisions and blaming an other.

192
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:08,711
Which is a very reliable tactic

193
00:09:08,711 --> 00:09:16,176
because precarity also correlates to increases of racism and xenophobia.

194
00:09:16,176 --> 00:09:19,346
There's still yet another group that tends to emerge

195
00:09:19,346 --> 00:09:22,891
in these historic moments of deep economic disparity,

196
00:09:22,891 --> 00:09:28,272
and they, too, see great benefit in autocracy and patronage

197
00:09:28,272 --> 00:09:31,692
and all sorts of other undemocratic practices.

198
00:09:31,692 --> 00:09:34,653
In the Gilded Age, we call them robber barons.

199
00:09:34,653 --> 00:09:37,573
In post-Soviet Russia, we call them oligarchs.

200
00:09:37,573 --> 00:09:40,075
Today, we call them billionaires,

201
00:09:40,075 --> 00:09:41,994
able to live above the law,

202
00:09:41,994 --> 00:09:44,705
exchanging donations for business monopolies

203
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:46,415
and government contracts.

204
00:09:46,415 --> 00:09:49,209
For very different reasons than the financially struggling,

205
00:09:49,209 --> 00:09:52,963
they see great benefit in autocracy.

206
00:09:52,963 --> 00:09:57,760
Economic inequality is like the heart disease of democracy,

207
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:00,512
so often ignored until it's too late.

208
00:10:00,512 --> 00:10:02,890
A threat to our very way of life.

209
00:10:02,890 --> 00:10:07,478
To fix it, we've got to stop distancing from each other and from the issue.

210
00:10:07,478 --> 00:10:09,396
We need to support candidates

211
00:10:09,396 --> 00:10:12,316
that will buttress the working and middle class,

212
00:10:12,316 --> 00:10:14,693
but we also need to know each other,

213
00:10:14,693 --> 00:10:17,321
to have empathy outside of our backyard,

214
00:10:17,321 --> 00:10:21,033
to cultivate compassion beyond our socioeconomic bracket.

215
00:10:21,033 --> 00:10:24,536
Because it's so easy to rail online at strangers

216
00:10:24,536 --> 00:10:28,332
and much harder to ignore the problems of our friends and neighbors.

217
00:10:28,332 --> 00:10:29,500
Thank you.

218
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:33,500
(Applause)
